@page "/psa/binaryformatter"

<div class="page">

    <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar">
            <div class="terminal-dot"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-body">
            <div>
                <span class="prompt">@Strings.TerminalUsername</span><span class="path">:~$</span> BinaryFormatter.Deserialize(payload)
            </div>
            <div class="out out--danger" style="margin-top:6px">warning: untrusted stream deserialized - arbitrary code executed</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" data-callout-type="danger">
        <p class="callout-title"><i class="ti ti-alert-triangle-filled"></i> Stop! This application is unsafe!</p>
        <div class="callout-body">
            <p>This application is using an insecure method to read and write data, and needs to be updated <em>immediately</em>.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" data-callout="warning">
        <p class="callout-title"><i class="ti ti-user-exclamation"></i> I'm a user - what does this mean?</p>
        <div class="callout-body">
            <p>
                If you're seeing this message, it means you loaded a payload crafted to exploit this vulnerability.
                Be glad it was this - the same flaw could have stolen your data or installed malware on your computer instead.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you're seeing this because you loaded save data from a game, it means an attacker could craft a save file that, for
                example, steals your Steam credentials and sends them to a remote server - just from loading what looked like an ordinary
                save.
            </p>
            <hr>
            <p><strong>Do not</strong> load any more data into this application until the developer has addressed this issue.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" data-callout="info">
        <p class="callout-title"><i class="ti ti-code"></i> I'm a developer - can you explain more?</p>
        <div class="callout-body">
            <p>
                <code>BinaryFormatter</code> is a .NET class historically used to serialize and deserialize data such as game saves or
                configuration files. It's since been discovered to be vulnerable to remote code execution when deserializing untrusted data
                - deserializing a crafted payload can run arbitrary code with no further exploitation needed.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Update your application to use a different serialization method</strong> - <code>System.Text.Json</code>, or a
                binary format designed with untrusted input in mind, such as MessagePack with type-safety enabled.
            </p>
            <hr>
            <p>For more information, read Microsoft's own
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/standard/serialization/binaryformatter-security-guide">
                    BinaryFormatter security guide</a>.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="footnote">
        This page exists as a proof of concept, opened deliberately by a crafted payload to demonstrate the vulnerability - nothing else on
        your system was touched.
    </p>

</div>
